    "etymology": {
     "text": "From Proto-Semitic *ḏV- (“this”). Cognates include Aramaic דִּי (dī) and Arabic ذا. Compare also the definite form הַזֶּה (hazé) and Arabic هٰذَا (hāḏā).",
     "src": "Wiktionary (kaikki.org)"
    },
    "funFacts": [
     "Hebrew ז regularly matches Arabic ذ (dh): זֶה ~ ذَا, זָהָב ~ ذَهَب (gold)."
    ],
    "pitfalls": [
     "Agreement: זֶה for a masculine noun, זֹאת for a feminine one, אֵלֶּה for plurals — זֶה בַּיִת, זֹאת דִּירָה, אֵלֶּה סְפָרִים.",
     "“This is a book” is זֶה סֵפֶר; “this book” is הַסֵּפֶר הַזֶּה — after a noun the demonstrative takes the article, and so does the noun.",
     "אֵלֶּה has one form for both genders: הַסְּפָרִים הָאֵלֶּה, הַמְּנוֹרוֹת הָאֵלֶּה."
    ],
    "subtleties": [
     "In casual speech זֶה is used for everything, even feminine and plural things: זֶה עַגְבָנִיָּה, זֶה טָעִים.",
     "Presenting something far away also uses זֶה: זֶה הַבַּיִת שֶׁלִּי “that (over there) is my house” — Hebrew does not need “that” here."
    ]
